1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Welcome back to The Explainer.
欢迎回到本期讲解。

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:10,300
Today we're taking hazard identification and transforming it from a simple gut feel exercise into a rigorous, literally life-saving discipline.
今天，我们要把危害识别从简单的凭直觉判断，转变为一门严谨的、实实在在能救命的学问。

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:16,800
Look, if you spend any time in a laboratory, you already know hazard ID is the absolute foundation of everything we do.
你看，只要你在实验室待过一段时间，你就知道危害识别是我们所做一切的根基。

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,200
You just can't control a hazard you haven't properly identified, right?
你根本无法控制一个你没有正确识别出来的危害，对吧？

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:27,800
So we're going to look at how mature world-class labs systematically hunt down what can hurt you long before it ever gets the chance.
所以，我们要看看成熟的世界一流实验室，是如何在危险有机会伤害你之前，就系统地把它们揪出来的。

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,300
Okay, let's kick things off with a pretty stark reminder from January 2010.
好，我们先从 2010 年 1 月的一个相当严酷的警示说起。

7
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:36,000
A grad student at Texas Tech was synthesizing a high-energy material.
德州理工大学的一名研究生当时正在合成一种高能材料。

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,700
Now, they were doing a hundred-fold scale-up from a well-established procedure.
他们在一个成熟的操作程序基础上，把规模放大了一百倍。

9
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:40,500
But what happened?
结果发生了什么？

10
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,200
A catastrophic detonation that literally severed the student's fingers.
一场灾难性的爆炸，直接炸断了这名学生的手指。

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,700
And the root cause is chilling.
而根本原因令人不寒而栗。

12
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:50,500
The institution had no documented hazard assessment for that massive scaled-up quantity.
该机构针对这种大幅放大后的用量，没有任何书面的危害评估。

13
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,800
See, they did a great job assessing the chemical toxicity, but completely missed the physical explosive hazard.
你看，他们对化学毒性做了很好的评估，却完全忽略了物理性的爆炸危害。

14
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,400
It's a really harsh lesson.
这是一个非常惨痛的教训。

15
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,500
If you only assess one type of danger, you're leaving yourself entirely vulnerable to the rest.
如果你只评估一种危险，那你对其余所有危险都毫无防备。

16
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:07,000
So, to make sure we avoid these invisible dangers, here's our game plan for today.
所以，为了确保我们避开这些看不见的危险，这是我们今天的计划。

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,400
One, we'll cover the vocabulary of risk.
第一，我们讲风险相关的术语。

18
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,900
Two, chemical and biological hazards.
第二，化学和生物危害。

19
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,600
Three, physical and radiological hazards.
第三，物理和辐射危害。

20
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,600
And finally, four, risk assessment methods.
最后，第四，风险评估方法。

21
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,900
All right, section one, the vocabulary of risk.
好，第一部分，风险相关的术语。

22
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:27,000
Honestly, mixing up these basic terms is probably the single most common reason an assessment goes off the rails.
说实话，混淆这些基本术语，可能是评估走偏的最常见原因。

23
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,800
So, it is absolutely crucial that we get this straight.
所以，把这些概念弄清楚是绝对关键的。

24
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,600
A hazard is simply what inherently exists.
危害就是本来就存在的东西。

25
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:37,400
It's the intrinsic property of a material, an energy form, or a process that can cause harm.
它是某种材料、某种能量形式或某个过程所固有的、可能造成伤害的属性。

26
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,400
Risk, on the other hand, well, that's what could actually happen.
而风险，嗯，是指实际上可能发生的情况。

27
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:47,900
Risk is the combination of the hazard, your exposure to it, meaning the route, the magnitude, and the duration of contact, and the actual consequence.
风险是危害、你对它的暴露——也就是接触的途径、程度和持续时间——以及实际后果三者的结合。

28
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:51,600
Simply put, if you manage the exposure, you manage the risk.
简单地说，管住了暴露，就管住了风险。

29
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:58,500
To put that into perspective, picture a bottle of concentrated sulfuric acid just sitting there in a sealed, flammable cabinet.
为了让你更直观地理解，想象一瓶浓硫酸静静地放在一个密闭的易燃品柜里。

30
00:01:58,500 --> 00:01:59,800
The inherent hazard?
它固有的危害呢？

31
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:05,300
Incredibly high, but your exposure is practically zero, so the risk is extremely low.
极高，但你的暴露几乎为零，所以风险极低。

32
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:11,100
Now, imagine an analyst taking that exact same acid and decanting it on the bench without a face shield.
现在，想象一名分析员拿着同一瓶酸，在没戴面罩的情况下在实验台上倾倒。

33
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:15,400
The inherent hazard hasn't changed one bit, but the risk just skyrocketed,
固有危害丝毫没有改变，但风险却飙升了，

34
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:19,800
because you've fundamentally changed the exposure route by ditching the protective gear.
因为你抛开了防护装备，从根本上改变了暴露途径。

35
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,200
The tricky part is, we're all human.
棘手的是，我们都是人。

36
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:27,200
Even the most experienced pros fall into cognitive traps when doing these assessments.
即使是经验最丰富的专业人员，在做这些评估时也会落入认知陷阱。

37
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:29,500
Take familiarity bias, for instance.
以熟悉偏差为例。

38
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,600
This happens when we start underrating hazards just because we've worked around them safely for years.
当我们仅仅因为多年来一直在这些危害周围安全地工作，就开始低估它们时，就会出现这种偏差。

39
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:40,700
Actually, because of this, highly experienced analysts can sometimes be at a higher risk than the nervous new guys.
事实上，正因为如此，经验丰富的分析员有时反而比那些紧张的新手风险更高。

40
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,100
Then there's anchoring.
然后是锚定效应。

41
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,700
That's when you glance at a safety data sheet, see a chemical listed as a moderate hazard,
就是当你瞥一眼安全技术说明书，看到某种化学品被列为中等危害，

42
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:53,500
and your brain just locks onto that, causing you to totally miss the severe chronic effects buried down on page 6.
你的大脑就锁定在这一点上，导致你完全忽略了埋在第 6 页的严重慢性效应。

43
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:58,000
Moving right along to section 2, chemical and biological hazards.
接下来是第二部分，化学和生物危害。

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,500
For chemicals, your safety data sheet, the SDS, is your primary evidence.
对于化学品，你的安全技术说明书，也就是 SDS，是你的主要依据。

45
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,700
It's the baseline inspectors we'll use to judge your lab practices.
它是检查员用来评判你们实验室操作的基准。

46
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:07,300
But here is the major catch.
但这里有一个重大问题。

47
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:11,000
The SDS only tells you about the chemical exactly as it was shipped.
SDS 只告诉你化学品在出厂时的状态。

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,600
It doesn't know about the unique in-situ mixtures you're cooking up or the scale effects of your specific process,
它不知道你在原位调配出的独特混合物，也不知道你具体工艺的规模效应，

49
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:23,400
because of this blind spot, maintaining a live, real-time chemical inventory is hands down your highest leverage safety investment.
正因为存在这个盲区，维护一份实时的化学品库存无疑是你杠杆效应最大的安全投资。

50
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:24,200
Think about it.
想一想。

51
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:28,500
A lab without one might spend 14 hours during a firewatch trying to figure out what's inside.
一个没有实时库存的实验室，在火灾值守期间可能要花 14 个小时才能弄清楚里面有什么。

52
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:30,100
A lab with a live inventory?
而一个有实时库存的实验室呢？

53
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:33,500
They hand that exact data to the fire marshal in 90 seconds flat.
他们只需 90 秒就能把准确的数据交给消防队长。

54
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:38,900
You've also got to keep a close eye on the silent chemical risks, like peroxide-forming solvents.
你还必须密切关注那些无声的化学风险，比如过氧化物形成溶剂。

55
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:42,800
These guys can actually become explosive just sitting on the shelf over time.
这类溶剂即使只是放在架子上，随着时间推移也可能变得具有爆炸性。

56
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,900
Take class B peroxide formers, like THF.
以 B 类过氧化物形成剂为例，比如 THF。

57
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:47,700
They are perfectly safe when dilute.
在稀溶液状态下，它们是完全安全的。

58
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:54,300
But let's say you find an aged bottle in the back of the cabinet and decide to concentrate or distill it without testing for peroxides first.
但假设你在柜子深处发现了一瓶放置已久的 THF，并决定在没有先检测过氧化物的情况下浓缩或蒸馏它。

59
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,700
You know, that is a literal textbook recipe for an explosion.
你知道，这简直就是教科书式的爆炸配方。

60
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:01,500
Now, biological hazards require a totally different framework.
现在，生物危害需要一个完全不同的框架。

61
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:08,000
We use the risk group, which ranges from 1 to 4, to tell us about the inherent danger of the infectious agent itself.
我们使用危险度分级，从 1 级到 4 级，来说明感染性病原体本身固有的危险程度。

62
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,400
For example, a harmless strain of E. coli is risk group 1, while something like Ebola is a risk group 4.
例如，一株无害的大肠杆菌属于危险度 1 级，而像埃博拉病毒这样的则属于危险度 4 级。

63
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:23,600
Meanwhile, the biosafety level, or BSL, tells you the specific containment facility, the practices, and the safety equipment you absolutely must use.
而生物安全等级，即 BSL，告诉你必须使用的具体防护设施、操作规范和安全设备。

64
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:30,700
The golden rule here is you have to perfectly match the lab's BSL to both the agent's risk group and the specific activity you're doing.
这里的黄金法则是，你必须让实验室的 BSL 与病原体的危险度分级以及你所进行的具体活动完全匹配。

65
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:35,500
The really critical takeaway here is that when you step into the biological hazard arena,
这里真正关键的要点是，当你踏入生物危害领域时，

66
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:40,800
you are stepping into some extremely strict, unforgiving, regulatory regimes.
你就进入了一些极其严格、毫不留情的监管体系。

67
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:47,100
Take the Federal Select Agent Program, which regulates severe threats like anthrax or botulinum neurotoxin.
以联邦管制病原体计划为例，它监管的是炭疽或肉毒神经毒素这类严重威胁。

68
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,500
These rules are unforgiving by design.
这些规定在设计上就是毫不留情的。

69
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:56,700
Just recently, a single misfiled vial of a toxin triggered an immediate, full-blown FBI and CDC investigation.
就在最近，仅仅一支归档错误的毒素小瓶就立即引发了 FBI 和 CDC 的全面调查。

70
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:01,000
It led to enforcement actions and reduced authorizations for the whole institution.
结果导致了执法行动，整个机构的授权范围也被缩减。

71
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,900
When it comes to this stuff, there is absolutely zero margin for inventory error.
在这类东西上，库存差错的容错余地绝对为零。

72
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:11,100
Alright, let's jump into Section 3, Physical and Radiological Hazards.
好，我们进入第三部分，物理和辐射危害。

73
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:15,500
I want you to look closely at this number, 6000 PSI.
我希望你仔细看看这个数字：6000 PSI。

74
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:19,600
That is the internal force sitting inside a typical compressed gas cylinder.
这就是一个典型压缩气瓶内部的压力。

75
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:25,100
To really put that into perspective for you, back in 1980 at NIST, a cylinder's valve broke off.
为了让你真正有直观的感受：1980 年在 NIST，一个气瓶的阀门断裂了。

76
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:30,100
The sudden release of all that pressure turned this heavy steel cylinder into an unguided missile
所有这些压力的突然释放，把这个沉重的钢瓶变成了一枚无制导导弹，

77
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,800
that punched through three solid concrete walls.
击穿了三堵实心混凝土墙。

78
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,800
No joke, three walls.
不是开玩笑，三堵墙。

79
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:39,000
Every single cylinder in your lab has to be treated with that exact level of respect,
你实验室里的每一个气瓶都必须得到同等程度的重视，

80
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,100
strapped, capped, and properly segregated at all times.
始终要固定好、戴好瓶帽，并妥善分类隔离。

81
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:45,600
Similarly, check out this number.
同样，看看这个数字。

82
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:53,600
Just one spilled liter of cryogenic liquid nitrogen instantly expands into about 700 liters of asphyxiating nitrogen gas.
仅仅洒出 1 升低温液氮，就会瞬间膨胀成大约 700 升令人窒息的氮气。

83
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:57,400
That's enough to displace the oxygen in a massive area in seconds.
这足以在几秒钟内置换掉一大片区域里的氧气。

84
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:02,000
It really goes to show that cold thermal hazards are often way sneakier than hot ones,
这确实说明，低温危害往往比高温危害隐蔽得多，

85
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:10,000
and it's exactly why any freezer room storing liquid nitrogen absolutely must have oxygen monitors with active, loud alarms installed.
这也正是为什么任何储存液氮的冷冻室都绝对必须安装带有有效、响亮报警功能的氧气监测仪。

86
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,200
And honestly, the bench is just packed with other highly energetic physical threats.
说实话，实验台上还充斥着其他高能量的物理威胁。

87
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:19,200
You got centrifuges acting like high-energy flywheels holding thousands of joules of stored energy.
离心机就像高能飞轮，储存着数千焦耳的能量。

88
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:25,100
If an imbalanced load causes a rotor-to-shear retaining bolt, that thing can eject straight through the housing.
如果不平衡的负载导致转子剪断固定螺栓，那东西就可能直接击穿外壳飞出去。

89
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:30,200
Then there are Class 3B and 4 lasers that can permanently blind a researcher in literal milliseconds
还有 3B 类和 4 类激光，如果研究人员没有佩戴光密度完全匹配的防护眼镜，

90
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:33,400
if they aren't wearing perfectly matched optical density eyewear.
它们能在短短几毫秒内造成永久性失明。

91
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:36,500
And we definitely can't forget radiological hazards.
我们当然也不能忘了辐射危害。

92
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:39,900
An improperly stored or scrapped radioactive calibration gauge?
一个储存不当或随意报废的放射性校准仪表？

93
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:45,800
That little mistake can easily cost an institution hundreds of thousands of dollars in regulatory fines and remediation.
这个小错误很容易让一个机构付出数十万美元的监管罚款和修复费用。

94
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,500
Physical hazards demand deep, systematic respect.
物理危害需要我们深刻、系统地加以重视。

95
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:54,900
Finally, bringing it all together in Section 4, Risk Assessment Methods.
最后，在第四部分把这一切整合起来：风险评估方法。

96
00:06:54,900 --> 00:07:02,500
For any non-routine task or brand-new technique, the Job Hazard Analysis, or JHA, is going to be your best friend.
对于任何非常规任务或全新的技术，作业危害分析，即 JHA，将是你最好的朋友。

97
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:06,500
It forces you to break the task down into clear, distinct steps.
它迫使你把任务分解成清晰、独立的步骤。

98
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:15,800
Then you identify the specific chemical, biological, physical, or ergonomic hazards at each one of those steps and map them to precise controls.
然后识别每个步骤中具体的化学、生物、物理或人体工学危害，并将它们对应到精确的控制措施上。

99
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:18,400
Look, this isn't just bureaucratic paperwork.
你看，这不只是官僚主义的文书工作。

100
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:24,700
A well-done JHA actually acts as the solid backbone for your lab's standard operating procedures.
一份做得好的 JHA 实际上是你们实验室标准操作程序的坚实骨架。

101
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:32,000
Now, if you need to prioritize where to spend your safety budget or time across a whole lab, you'll want to use a risk matrix.
现在，如果你需要在整个实验室范围内确定安全预算或时间的优先投向，你就要用到风险矩阵。

102
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,500
By plotting likelihood against severity, you essentially build a visual heat map.
通过把可能性与严重性对应绘制，你基本上就构建了一张直观的热力图。

103
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:41,300
It instantly highlights exactly where catastrophic severity meets high likelihood.
它能立即凸显出灾难性严重程度与高可能性重叠的地方。

104
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:48,700
That tells you precisely where you need to take immediate action, like, say, installing a new glove box or mandating fire-resistant gear,
这能准确告诉你哪里需要立即采取行动，比如说安装一台新的手套箱或强制要求穿戴阻燃装备，

105
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:51,700
versus the lower risks that just need regular monitoring.
而哪些较低的风险只需定期监控即可。

106
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:55,400
For the really complex stuff, mature labs use advanced systems.
对于真正复杂的情况，成熟的实验室会使用先进的体系。

107
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:59,400
They use control banding to bridge the gap when toxicology data is incomplete,
当毒理学数据不完整时，他们使用控制分级法来弥补空白，

108
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:03,100
assigning general control packages based on known hazard families.
根据已知的危害类别分配通用的控制措施包。

109
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:10,000
For ultra-high-sticks processes, they might use FMEA to meticulously calculate risk priority numbers for every conceivable failure mode.
对于风险极高的工艺，他们可能会使用 FMEA，为每一种可以想到的失效模式精确计算风险优先数。

110
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,900
And, I gotta say, executives absolutely love the bowtie analysis.
而且，我得说，高管们非常喜欢蝴蝶结分析。

111
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:16,900
It brilliantly visualizes a central hazardous event,
它出色地把一个核心危险事件可视化，

112
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:23,800
mapping out all your preventative barriers on the left and mitigation barriers on the right, all on one easy-to-digest page.
在左边列出所有预防性屏障，在右边列出所有减缓性屏障，全都在一页易于理解的纸上。

113
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:28,500
But listen, ultimately, an undocumented assessment is an invisible assessment.
但请注意，归根结底，没有记录的评估就是看不见的评估。

114
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:33,600
In the eyes of OSHO and accrediting bodies, if you didn't write it down, it simply didn't happen.
在 OSHA 和认证机构看来，如果你没有把它写下来，那它就根本没有发生过。

115
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:39,600
You have got to sign, date, and securely link your hazard findings to your lab's active workflows,
你必须签字、注明日期，并把危害识别结果牢固地关联到实验室正在运行的工作流程、

116
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:42,600
training records, and standard operating procedures.
培训记录和标准操作程序上。

117
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:48,600
An assessment sitting locked away in some dusty, digital folder serves absolutely no one at the bench.
一份锁在某个落满灰尘的电子文件夹里的评估，对实验台前的任何人都毫无用处。

118
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:52,900
So, I want to leave you with the thought to really challenge your own familiarity bias.
所以，我想留给你一个想法，来真正挑战你自己的熟悉偏差。

119
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:57,100
When you consider the invisible physical forces, the unforgiving biological agents,
当你考虑到你所处环境中那些看不见的物理力量、毫不留情的生物病原体

120
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:00,700
and the complex systemic threats right there in your environment,
以及复杂的系统性威胁时，

121
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:05,900
how would you systematically rate the actual risk of your very last laboratory experiment?
你会如何系统地评估你最近一次实验的实际风险？

122
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:09,000
I really encourage you to look at your bench tomorrow with totally fresh eyes,
我真心鼓励你明天用全新的眼光看看你的实验台，

123
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,700
step past those biases, and rigorously ask yourself, what hazards might I be missing?
跨越那些偏差，严格地问问自己：我可能遗漏了哪些危害？

124
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:17,000
Thank you so much for joining me on This Explainer, and please stay safe out there.
非常感谢你收看本期讲解，请注意安全。
